WEBVTT

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:04.250
<v Ira Glass>Tuesday, Tucson, Arizona.
<v Ira Glass>周二，亚利桑那州图森市。

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:05.960
<v Sarah Bromer>How are you feeling?
<v Sarah Bromer>你感觉怎么样？

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.960
<v Rediet Seifu>I'm scared.
<v Rediet Seifu>我很害怕。

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:23.820
<v Ira Glass>Rediet Seifu moved to the United States from Ethiopia four months ago. She's learning English. And once a week, one of her teachers from the City High School in Tuscon, Sarah Bromer, takes her to Ike's coffee shop to practice a simple English conversation.
<v Ira Glass>Rediet Seifu 四个月前从埃塞俄比亚搬到了美国。她正在学习英语。每周一次，她的一位老师，来自图森市城市高中的 Sarah Bromer 会带她去 Ike 咖啡店练习简单的英语对话。

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:35.600
<v Ira Glass>It goes like this-- Rediet orders tea. That's it, actually. That's the whole thing. Usually, they walk past the door four or five times before she even gets the courage to go inside to do this. And they rehearse beforehand.
<v Ira Glass>流程是这样的——Rediet 点杯茶。其实就是这样。整个过程就这么多。通常，她们会在门口徘徊四五次，她才有勇气走进去。而且她们会事先排练。

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:40.710
<v Sarah Bromer>So she's going to go-- she calls everybody hon. Do you know what that means? Like honey?
<v Sarah Bromer>她会去——她管所有人都叫“亲爱的”（hon）。你知道那是什么意思吗？就像“蜂蜜”（honey）一样？

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:41.510
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的。

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:47.330
<v Ira Glass>Today, for the very first time, Ms. Bromer is making Rediet order without her help.
<v Ira Glass>今天，Ms. Bromer 第一次让 Rediet 在没有她帮助的情况下点餐。

00:00:47.330 --> 00:00:57.650
<v Sarah Bromer>She might say, hey, hon, what do you want? Or I bet she'll say, hey, hon, what can I get for you? You'll say--
<v Sarah Bromer>她可能会说，嘿，亲爱的，你要什么？或者我敢打赌她会说，嘿，亲爱的，我能为你点些什么？你会说——

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.520
<v Rediet Seifu>I would like a tea, please.
<v Rediet Seifu>我想来杯茶，谢谢。

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.150
<v Sarah Bromer>And then what if she says, what? What? Pardon me?
<v Sarah Bromer>那如果她说，什么？什么？请再说一遍？

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:09.090
<v Rediet Seifu>A tea. You confused me.
<v Rediet Seifu>一杯茶。你把我搞糊涂了。

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:18.700
<v Ira Glass>Ms. Bromer's actually teasing Rediet by saying, "What?" She knows that this is Rediet's biggest fear, that people will not understand her, that they're going to say, what? What? What was that?
<v Ira Glass>Ms. Bromer 其实是在逗 Rediet，故意说“什么？”。她知道这是 Rediet 最大的恐惧，怕别人听不懂她说话，怕他们会问，什么？什么？你说什么？

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.850
<v Sarah Bromer>Well, that's what you're afraid of, right?
<v Sarah Bromer>好吧，这就是你害怕的事情，对吧？

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.680
<v Rediet Seifu>You confused me.
<v Rediet Seifu>你把我搞糊涂了。

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:31.220
<v Sarah Bromer>You're afraid, right, you're going to say, can I have a tea? And then she's going to go, what? What?
<v Sarah Bromer>你很害怕，对吧，怕你会说，能给我杯茶吗？然后她会说，什么？什么？

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.530
<v Rediet Seifu>Tea.
<v Rediet Seifu>茶。

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:38.840
<v Sarah Bromer>No, you're saying it just fine. She's going to know what you want. I'm just kidding. You'll say, I want a tea.
<v Sarah Bromer>不，你说的挺好的。她会知道你想要什么的。我只是开个玩笑。你会说，我想要杯茶。

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:42.000
<v Rediet Seifu>OK. I want a tea.
<v Rediet Seifu>好的。我想要杯茶。

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.070
<v Sarah Bromer>Good. She'll say, what kind do you want?
<v Sarah Bromer>很好。她会说，你想要哪种？

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.000
<v Rediet Seifu>Honeybush tea.
<v Rediet Seifu>蜜树茶（Honeybush tea）。

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.920
<v Sarah Bromer>Huh?
<v Sarah Bromer>啊？

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.090
<v Sarah Bromer>[GIGGLING]
<v Sarah Bromer>[咯咯笑]

00:01:50.090 --> 00:01:55.770
<v Ira Glass>OK, they practice a few more times. And then, finally, Rediet approaches the counter.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，她们又练习了几次。然后，终于，Rediet 走到柜台前。

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.340
<v Rediet Seifu>Hello.
<v Rediet Seifu>你好。

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:57.820
<v Barista>Hi.
<v Barista>嗨。

00:01:57.820 --> 00:01:58.780
<v Man 1>Have a good one.
<v Man 1>祝你过得愉快。

00:01:58.780 --> 00:01:59.740
<v Barista>Bye, guys.
<v Barista>再见，各位。

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:01.430
<v Rediet Seifu>I would like a tea, please.
<v Rediet Seifu>我想来杯茶，谢谢。

00:02:01.430 --> 00:02:02.860
<v Barista>I'm sorry?
<v Barista>抱歉，你说什么？

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:10.960
<v Ira Glass>OK, I just have to stop the tape right there. Did you hear? It happened. Exactly what she feared. The lady didn't understand her.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，我得在这里暂停一下录音。你听到了吗？发生了。正是她所担心的。女士没听懂她的话。

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.720
<v Ira Glass>And listen how she handles this. She's so good. Let's take that from the top again.
<v Ira Glass>听听她是怎么处理的。她做得太棒了。让我们重新来一遍。

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.740
<v Rediet Seifu>I would like a tea, please.
<v Rediet Seifu>我想来杯茶，谢谢。

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:18.320
<v Barista>I'm sorry?
<v Barista>抱歉，你说什么？

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:20.180
<v Rediet Seifu>I would like a tea, please.
<v Rediet Seifu>我想来杯茶，谢谢。

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.040
<v Barista>Sure.
<v Barista>好的。

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:22.510
<v Sarah Bromer>Hot tea.
<v Sarah Bromer>热茶。

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:23.440
<v Barista>Hot tea?
<v Barista>热茶？

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:23.910
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的。

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:25.310
<v Barista>Do you know which kind you'd like, sweetheart?
<v Barista>你知道想要哪种吗，亲爱的？

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:26.560
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah, honeybush tea.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的，蜜树茶。

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:26.830
<v Barista>Honeybush?
<v Barista>蜜树茶？

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:27.750
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的。

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:28.670
<v Barista>Anything else?
<v Barista>还要别的吗？

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:29.130
<v Sarah Bromer>No.
<v Sarah Bromer>没有。

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.050
<v Barista>OK.
<v Barista>好的。

00:02:30.050 --> 00:02:33.480
<v Ira Glass>She doesn't giggle. She doesn't panic. She's perfect.
<v Ira Glass>她没有咯咯笑。她没有慌乱。她做得非常完美。

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.210
<v Barista>$1.69.
<v Barista>1.69 美元。

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:39.640
<v Ira Glass>It's her first time buying something by herself in the US.
<v Ira Glass>这是她第一次在美国独自购买东西。

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:42.110
<v Barista>And $0.06 is your change, honey.
<v Barista>这是找你的 0.06 美元零钱，亲爱的。

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:49.070
<v Ira Glass>Welcome to America, Rediet. You bought something. You're engaging in commerce. You're talking to the lady at the counter.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎来到美国，Rediet。你买到东西了。你在进行商业交易。你在和柜台后面的女士交流。

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:55.940
<v Ira Glass>We are so glad to have you here. Have some tea. You'll be paying for it yourself.
<v Ira Glass>我们很高兴有你在这里。喝点茶吧。是你自己付的钱哦。

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:56.780
<v Rediet Seifu>I'm not scared.
<v Rediet Seifu>我不害怕了。

00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:57.740
<v Sarah Bromer>You're not?
<v Sarah Bromer>你不怕了吗？

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.160
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的。

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.410
<v Sarah Bromer>Really? You felt brave?
<v Sarah Bromer>真的吗？你觉得勇敢了吗？

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:04.270
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的。

00:03:04.270 --> 00:03:07.670
<v Sarah Bromer>Well, I think you did a really good job.
<v Sarah Bromer>嗯，我觉得你做得非常好。

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:19.460
<v Rediet Seifu>Yeah, I think. Yeah.
<v Rediet Seifu>是的，我也觉得。是的。

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:45.940
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. This week on our radio program, we're trying something unusual for us. This is the second time we've done this. Instead of our usual picking a theme and finding stories on the theme, the theme this week will simply be the week, the week that we all just lived through, these past seven days. Everything on the show has just happened, including stories that are so small and personal-- like, you know, Rediet ordering her first tea-- that they would never belong on a regular news show.
<v Ira Glass>我是 Ira Glass。本周在我们的广播节目中，我们尝试了一些不寻常的做法。这是我们第二次这么做。我们没有像往常那样挑选一个主题并寻找相关故事，本周的主题就是这周本身，我们刚刚度过的这一周，过去的七天。节目中的一切都是刚刚发生的，包括那些非常细微和私人的故事——比如，Rediet 点她的第一杯茶——这些故事通常不会出现在常规新闻节目中。

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:59.350
<v Ira Glass>And we have stories that are bigger, too, like this next woman, whose husband is Syrian. They lived there again in Syria till January, when it got too unstable. And she came to America, where she grew up. And then something bad happened.
<v Ira Glass>我们也有一些更重大的故事，比如下一位女士，她的丈夫是叙利亚人。他们住在叙利亚，直到一月份，局势变得太不稳定。她回到了她长大的美国。然后发生了一些糟糕的事情。

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:13.990
<v Woman>I don't know. Really, I don't believe this could be happening. It seems like something that would happen in movies or in a book. We're just normal people.
<v Woman>我不知道。真的，我不相信这种事会发生。这看起来像是电影或书里才会有的情节。我们只是普通人。

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:30.210
<v Ira Glass>Her husband's still in Syria. He owns some businesses there. And two of their employees were taken hostage by the rebel forces who are fighting President Bashar al-Assad. President Obama has called for him to step down, of course, on Thursday. The Pentagon warned him not to use chemical weapons.
<v Ira Glass>她丈夫还在叙利亚。他在那里经营一些生意。他们的两名员工被反对派武装绑架了，那些反对派正在与巴沙尔·阿萨德总统作战。奥巴马总统周四当然呼吁他下台。五角大楼警告他不要使用化学武器。

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.000
<v Ira Glass>Did you talk to your husband today?
<v Ira Glass>你今天跟你丈夫通话了吗？

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:33.160
<v Woman>Yeah, I did.
<v Woman>是的，我打了。

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.400
<v Ira Glass>What's the very latest?
<v Ira Glass>最新情况是什么？

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:44.080
<v Woman>He told me this morning they said that they would accept $80,000, and my husband said OK.
<v Woman>他今天早上告诉我，他们说他们会接受 8 万美元，我丈夫说可以。

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:46.140
<v Ira Glass>And you and I are talking on Wednesday.
<v Ira Glass>我们是在周三通话的。

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:58.090
<v Woman>Yes. Yeah, I mean, I think their objective is to get the money and hopefully not to kill somebody in between.
<v Woman>是的。我是说，我想他们的目标是拿到钱，希望在此期间不要杀人。

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:05.170
<v Ira Glass>And it must make you feel so strange to feel like, oh, and these people who you guys are negotiating with, those are the US allies in the war.
<v Ira Glass>当你觉得，哦，这些人，你们正在谈判的对象，居然是美国在战争中的盟友，这一定让你感觉很奇怪吧。

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:17.170
<v Woman>Yeah. They're trying to clean up their act and get rid of this type of stuff, or appear like that. And I don't know--
<v Woman>是的。他们正试图洗心革面，摆脱这种事情，或者至少看起来是这样。我不知道——

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:23.900
<v Ira Glass>So have you spent most of this week just, like, on pins and needles just to hear what the next thing is with these employees?
<v Ira Glass>所以这周大部分时间你是不是都像坐在针毡上一样，就为了听关于这些员工的最新消息？

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:40.140
<v Woman>Yeah. I haven't slept because of this. So hopefully-- I don't know if you are familiar with how things work in the Middle East, but everything always takes forever.
<v Woman>是的。我因此彻夜难眠。所以希望——我不知道你是否熟悉中东的事情运作方式，但一切总是需要很长时间。

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:47.100
<v Naderev Sano>We don't really have 25 days. We have a few precious hours left. We are at a critical juncture.
<v Naderev Sano>我们其实没有 25 天。我们只剩下几个宝贵的小时了。我们处于关键时刻。

00:05:47.100 --> 00:06:04.590
<v Ira Glass>In Doha, Qatar, this week, at the latest United Nations Climate Change Conference, the main goal that they were trying to achieve seemed incredibly modest. The things that the world originally negotiated in 1997 in Kyoto about climate change are about to expire. If all goes as planned, the delegates here hope to recommit to them.
<v Ira Glass>本周在卡塔尔多哈举行的最新一届联合国气候变化大会上，他们试图实现的主要目标看起来极其微小。世界 1997 年在京都就气候变化最初谈判达成的协议即将到期。如果一切按计划进行，这里的代表们希望重新承诺这些协议。

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:40.240
<v Ira Glass>But their bigger mission-- for instance, limiting the increase in world temperature to 2 degrees Celsius, which the world's governments committed to in 2009 in Copenhagen-- is starting to seem impossible, meaning more hurricane Sandys, more drought, more floods more frequently. A typhoon called Bopha hit the Philippines on Tuesday with a death toll that'll probably be twice that of Hurricane Sandy. This man speaking is the lead negotiator from the Philippines, Naderev Sano, giving a speech that becomes, as you'll hear, very unusual for this deeply bureaucratic, diplomatic setting.
<v Ira Glass>但他们更大的使命——例如，将世界气温上升限制在 2 摄氏度以内，这是世界各国政府 2009 年在哥本哈根承诺的——看起来开始变得不可能了，这意味着更多的桑迪飓风、更多的干旱、更频繁的洪水。周二，一场名为“宝霞”（Bopha）的台风袭击了菲律宾，死亡人数可能将是飓风桑迪的两倍。正在发言的这位男士是菲律宾首席谈判代表 Naderev Sano，正如你将听到的，他在这个极其官僚和外交的场合发表了一场非常不同寻常的演讲。

00:06:40.240 --> 00:07:03.420
<v Naderev Sano>An important backdrop for my delegation is the profound impacts of climate change that we are already confronting. And as we see here, every single hour, even as we vacillate and procrastinate here, we are suffering. Madam Chair, we have never had a typhoon like Bopha, which has wreaked havoc in a part of the country that has never seen a storm like this in half a century.
<v Naderev Sano>对于我的代表团来说，一个重要的背景是我们已经面临的气候变化的深远影响。正如我们在这里看到的，每一小时，即使我们在这里犹豫不决和拖延，我们都在受苦。主席女士，我们从未经历过像“宝霞”这样的台风，它在我国一个半世纪以来从未见过此类风暴的地区造成了严重破坏。

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:26.640
<v Naderev Sano>Finally, Madam Chair, I'm making an urgent appeal, not as a negotiator, not as a leader of my delegation, but as a Filipino. I appeal to the whole world. I appeal to the leaders from all over the world to open our eyes to the stark reality that we face. I appeal to ministers.
<v Naderev Sano>最后，主席女士，我发出紧急呼吁，不是作为谈判代表，也不是作为代表团团长，而是作为一名菲律宾人。我向全世界呼吁。我呼吁世界各地的领导人睁开眼睛，面对我们所处的残酷现实。我呼吁部长们。

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:56.770
<v Naderev Sano>The outcome of our work is not about what our political masters want. It is about what is demanded of us by 7 billion people. I appeal to all-- please, no more delays. No more excuses. Please, let Doha be remembered as the place where we found the political will to turn things around.
<v Naderev Sano>我们工作的成果不是关于我们的政治主人们想要什么。而是关于 70 亿人民对我们的要求。我呼吁所有人——请，不要再推迟了。不要再有借口了。请让多哈被铭记为我们找到政治意愿来扭转局面的地方。

00:07:56.770 --> 00:08:03.690
<v Michael Mcgill>No choo-choo out of the train. No train whistle in the train. No doorbells.
<v Michael Mcgill>火车没有呜呜声。火车上没有火车汽笛声。没有门铃。

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:31.680
<v Ira Glass>As we poured gases into the atmosphere that are slowly heating the planet, threatening our very existence, we also put on plays for children and the disabled this week. In Montana, the Missoula Community Theatre prepared a special performance of the musical Miracle on 34th Street for people on the autism spectrum. This meant no sudden loud noises, lower lights, volunteers with glow sticks signaling when to applaud, and lower sound levels in general. Director Michael McGill discussed the changes with the cast before the show.
<v Ira Glass>当我们把气体排入大气层，缓慢地加热地球，威胁到我们的生存时，我们这周也为儿童和残疾人举办了戏剧表演。在蒙大拿州，米苏拉社区剧院为患有自闭症谱系的人们准备了一场特别的音乐剧《34 街的奇迹》。这意味着没有突然的巨响，灯光调暗，志愿者用荧光棒示意何时鼓掌，整体音量降低。导演 Michael McGill 在演出前与演员讨论了这些变化。

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.830
<v Michael Mcgill>Young people, when you come out for Donald Duck, you come out quickly, but you don't scream.
<v Michael Mcgill>年轻人，当唐老鸭出场时，你们要快速出场，但不要尖叫。

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:41.590
<v Woman>Just know that Mrs. Sawyer's going to be very quiet in the trial scene.
<v Woman>只要知道 Sawyer 太太在审判场景中会非常安静就行了。

00:08:41.590 --> 00:08:43.690
<v Woman>[GROANING]
<v Woman>[呻吟声]

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:52.920
<v Emcee>OK, here we go. Our first group coming up. We've got Hype-- Hyphen. Y'all give it up for Hyphen. They're doing some step for us today. Give it up, give it up, give it up, give it up.
<v Emcee>好了，我们开始吧。我们的第一组要上场了。我们有 Hype——Hyphen。大家为 Hyphen 加油。他们今天为我们展示一些步法。加油，加油，加油，加油。

00:08:52.920 --> 00:09:07.200
<v Ira Glass>At the Best of Both Worlds Dance and Step Competition in Charlottesville, Virginia, Akayla Brown, Aquasia Baker, and Destiny Grady, ages 10, 10, and 11, told reporter Eric Mennel that they were confident of winning, even though they were competing against college kids.
<v Ira Glass>在弗吉尼亚州夏洛茨维尔举行的“最佳世界舞蹈和步法大赛”上，10 岁、10 岁和 11 岁的 Akayla Brown、Aquasia Baker 和 Destiny Grady 告诉记者 Eric Mennel，她们对获胜很有信心，尽管她们是在和大学生比赛。

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:18.200
<v Akayla Brown>And I think they'd be like, oh, we can just beat the little kids and this and that. They just think that they can just get in our Kool-Aid when they don't even know the flavor.
