00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:54.560
<v Hyder Akbar>And me and Sartor are kind of just looking at the car on fire, and we're like, what the hell? And we're looking at it, and Josef's screaming, get the hell out of here! Get the hell out of here!
<v Hyder Akbar>我和 Sartor 只是看着车着火，我们想，到底怎么回事？我们看着它，Josef 在尖叫，快他妈离开这里！快他妈离开这里！

00:18:54.560 --> 00:19:08.830
<v Hyder Akbar>And I just looked at Sartor, and I was like, dude, my laptop bag's in there. Everything's in there. So we both run in. We open the door to go inside to grab the laptop bag out of it, with my laptop and other stuff in there.
<v Hyder Akbar>我看着 Sartor，我说，伙计，我的笔记本电脑包在里面。所有东西都在里面。所以我们俩都跑过去。我们打开门进去拿笔记本包，我的笔记本电脑和其他东西都在里面。

00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:24.380
<v Hyder Akbar>And so as we do that, they start firing again. Luckily, the car covered us. And the bullets just hit the back of the car as we were on the side of the car trying to get the laptop bag out. We managed to get the laptop bag out. And then I'm kind of hiding behind the car.
<v Hyder Akbar>当我们这样做时，他们又开始开火了。幸运的是，车身保护了我们。当我们试图在车边拿笔记本包时，子弹击中了车尾。我们成功拿到了笔记本包。然后我就躲在车后面。

00:19:24.380 --> 00:19:36.520
<v Hyder Akbar>I take out my iPhone, because I'm like, OK, I've got to record this a little bit, at least, for you guys. I was like, I want to record this thing. This is an interesting thing. I look at my iPhone. It has, like, 1% battery, 2%. I'm like, [BLEEP].
<v Hyder Akbar>我掏出我的 iPhone，因为我想，好吧，我至少得为你们录下来。我想，我要录下这个东西。这是一件有趣的事。我看着我的 iPhone。它大概只有 1% 的电量，2%。我想，[脏话]。

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:48.140
<v Hyder Akbar>So I have this on record, and I see Josef just come running back down. And it was kind of funny. He's like, what are you doing? I'm like, I need to record the car, I need to record the car for the thing. Remember, we came here to do the radio thing.
<v Hyder Akbar>所以我录下来了，然后我看到 Josef 跑回来了。挺滑稽的。他说，你在干什么？我说，我得录下这辆车，我得为那个节目录下这辆车。记得吗，我们来这里是为了做广播节目的。

00:19:48.140 --> 00:20:02.390
<v Hyder Akbar>And he cursed you guys out and punched the hood. And basically, he said, F This American Life. What the hell are you going to do with This American Life when you're dead? And he started dragging me away.
<v Hyder Akbar>他骂了你们，并重重地打了一下车盖。基本上，他说，去他妈的《美国生活》。你死后对《美国生活》能做什么？他开始把我拉走。

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:09.560
<v Hyder Akbar>And right then, the phone turned off too. The phone turned off too, so it didn't save the recording, either. And he's dragging me away. As he's dragging me away--
<v Hyder Akbar>就在那时，手机也关机了。手机也关机了，所以录音也没保存下来。他把我拉走。当他把我拉走时——

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:27.780
<v Ira Glass>OK, soon after this in the conversation, I let Hyder know that if he is ever again in any situation where he might get killed, it is OK if he runs away and stops recording for the radio show. So he explained that they went around the bend. They saw two civilian cars stopped there. They were still being shot at. So they got into those cars. They got away.
<v Ira Glass>好的，在谈话的稍后，我让 Hyder 知道，如果他再次遇到任何可能被杀的情况，他完全可以逃跑，停止为广播节目录音。所以他解释说，他们绕过了弯道。他们看到两辆民用汽车停在那里。他们仍在被射击。所以他们钻进那些车里。他们逃走了。

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:45.170
<v Ira Glass>This was the first time, Hyder said, that somebody specifically tried to kill him. And he thinks that they wanted him because they were letting other cars pass by on the road, and they specifically tried to stop his car. But who was it who was targeting him? And why were they targeting him? That wasn't clear to him at all.
<v Ira Glass>Hyder 说，这是第一次有人专门试图杀他。他认为他们想要他是因为他们让其他车辆从路上经过，却专门试图拦住他的车。但究竟是谁在针对他？为什么针对他？对他来说根本不清楚。

00:20:45.170 --> 00:20:53.220
<v Hyder Akbar>That's something I'm going to have to think about and figure out tonight, in the next couple of days.
<v Hyder Akbar>这是我今晚和接下来几天得好好想想并弄清楚的事情。

00:20:53.220 --> 00:21:02.230
<v Ira Glass>Well, what are the possibilities? List through right now, tick off a couple of possibilities, just so I understand. What could it be?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，可能性有哪些？列举一下，快速列举几种可能性，这样我能理解。会是什么呢？

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:39.690
<v Hyder Akbar>It could have been the Taliban. It could have been the Taliban, who might have been following me a lot more closely than I realized, following my locations, where I'm spending the night, where I'm living. They definitely knew exactly where I was. I can't think of anything personal, in terms of any personal feuds, et cetera. So it'd be most likely the Taliban or people in Kunal that are with the insurgency that don't see me, I guess, as a threat.
<v Hyder Akbar>可能是塔利班。可能是塔利班，他们可能比我意识到的跟踪得更紧，跟踪我的位置，我在哪里过夜，在哪里生活。他们绝对知道我确切在哪里。我想不出任何个人的事，比如任何私人恩怨等等。所以最可能是塔利班或库纳尔那些反叛势力的人，我猜他们并不认为我是个威胁。

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:53.550
<v Ira Glass>And Hyder, is it as simple as you and your dad aren't necessarily so crazy about the current government in Afghanistan, but they know you definitely do not like the Taliban? And is it as simple as that?
<v Ira Glass>Hyder，是不是很简单，你和你父亲不一定那么喜欢阿富汗现政府，但他们确实知道你绝对不喜欢塔利班？就是这么简单吗？

00:21:53.550 --> 00:22:34.370
<v Hyder Akbar>Almost as simple as that. The one thing I would add onto that is they could see us as being even more of a threat because we're not that close to this government anymore, because we don't approve of many things that are going on in the government. They can sideline a lot of other people very easily by saying, oh, these guys are corrupted individuals, involved with the government, fattening their pockets. But when they see me driving around in a [BLEEP] Corolla and just talking to people, engaging them, and being pretty sincere in my efforts, that's seen as much more of a threat, because they could delegitimize the Afghan government and the officials involved with them much easier.
<v Hyder Akbar>几乎就这么简单。我要补充的一点是，他们可能会认为我们是更大的威胁，因为我们不再与这个政府那么亲近了，因为我们不赞成政府中发生的许多事情。他们可以很容易地通过说，哦，这些人是腐败分子，参与政府，中饱私囊，来边缘化许多其他人。但当他们看到我开着一辆[脏话]花冠车到处走，只是和人们谈话，与他们接触，并且在努力方面相当真诚时，这被视为更大的威胁，因为他们可以更容易地使阿富汗政府和参与其中的官员失去合法性。

00:22:34.370 --> 00:22:53.240
<v Ira Glass>And it's not crazy to think that Hyder is now at a point where he would be worth killing. His family's from Kunar. His father and uncle were in the insurgency in Kunar that drove out the Soviets in the 1980s. When the current Afghan government came to power after September 11, Hyder's dad was appointed governor of Kunar. They have friends there. They have a base of support.
<v Ira Glass>认为 Hyder 现在处于一个值得被杀的地步并不疯狂。他的家族来自库纳尔。他的父亲和叔叔在 20 世纪 80 年代在库纳尔的反叛势力中赶走了苏联人。9/11 事件后，当现任阿富汗政府上台时，Hyder 的父亲被任命为库纳尔省长。他们在那里有朋友。他们有支持基础。

00:22:53.240 --> 00:23:11.590
<v Ira Glass>And as the Americans pull out in the next two years, there's going to be a power vacuum in the country. And people are getting in position for that, including Hyder. He is setting himself up to do politics. The way that he explains it, he says he wants to be the Afghan equivalent of a congressman, though with a militia. You need a militia, because it's Afghanistan.
<v Ira Glass>随着美国人在未来两年撤出，这个国家将出现权力真空。人们正在为此做准备，包括 Hyder。他正在为从政做准备。按照他的解释，他说他想成为阿富汗版的国会议员，尽管需要民兵。你需要民兵，因为这是阿富汗。

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:40.830
<v Ira Glass>We talked for a while. Hyder insisted that he is planning on going back to Kunar soon. And he's going to do it just like he did this time, in another beat-up old car, to prove that they didn't scare him, to prove that he wasn't going to stay away, which I really hope that his mom and his dad and his uncle talk him out of. And then we finally got to the reason for his trip in the first place, the story that he was so excited to bring back, that he ended up risking his life for, of what was happening in the province.
<v Ira Glass>我们谈了一会儿。Hyder 坚持说他计划很快回库纳尔。他会像这次一样，开着另一辆破旧的老车去，证明他们没吓倒他，证明他不会远离，我真的希望他的妈妈、爸爸和叔叔能劝阻他。最后我们终于回到了他旅行的初衷，那个他如此兴奋地带回来，最终为此冒着生命危险的故事，关于该省正在发生什么。

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:45.670
<v Ira Glass>So just review the highlights for me. So let's talk about what you saw when you were there. What's interesting?
<v Ira Glass>所以请为我回顾一下亮点。让我们谈谈你在那里看到了什么。有什么有趣的？

00:23:45.670 --> 00:23:56.490
<v Hyder Akbar>OK, so we drove up there. We drove up there yesterday. And we went into the Pesh Valley.
<v Hyder Akbar>好吧，我们开车上去了。我们昨天开车上去了。我们进入了佩什山谷（Pesh Valley）。

00:23:56.490 --> 00:24:11.240
<v Hyder Akbar>It's actually kind of funny. So the Pesh Valley's sort of like the valley of death. It was the place that was considered very dangerous. And some interesting new dynamics have emerged there with the ANA, the Afghan National Army.
<v Hyder Akbar>其实挺逗的。佩什山谷有点像死亡之谷。它曾经被认为非常危险。那里出现了一些有趣的动态，关于阿富汗国民军（ANA）。

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:14.750
<v Ira Glass>Again, this is the government's army. This for the government of Afghanistan.
<v Ira Glass>重申一下，这是政府的军队。这是为了阿富汗政府。

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:36.340
<v Hyder Akbar>The ANA, the Afghan National Army, is in control there now. The Americans have pulled out. What's going on because of that is that a lot of people, especially the civilian population, is siding with the ANA now. The ANA is just culturally much more aware. They've been able to make inroads amongst the population.
<v Hyder Akbar>阿富汗国民军现在控制那里。美国人撤走了。因为这个，很多事情正在发生，很多人，特别是平民人口，现在站在国民军这一边。国民军在文化上更具意识。他们能够深入人口中。

00:24:36.340 --> 00:25:01.140
<v Hyder Akbar>They've been able to push the Taliban out, especially from the main roads. I was driving by areas where people used to get stopped and beheaded, government officials or those considered working with the Americans, driving past all of these potholes in the road where they had laid down IEDs. And that was fine. It was fine. And we're all taking in the scenery.
<v Hyder Akbar>他们能够把塔利班赶走，特别是在主要道路上。我开车经过人们曾经被拦住并斩首的地方，政府官员或那些被认为与美国人合作的人，开车经过路上那些他们埋下简易爆炸装置（IED）的坑坑洼洼。那没问题。没问题。我们都在欣赏风景。

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:22.870
<v Hyder Akbar>And it was a very heartwarming story, actually. I was actually very excited to come back. I was kind of aware, I was like, OK, maybe good news is not always that fun to report about. I was like, but it'll be exciting to finally come back. And I was very hopeful and feeling pretty jolly.
<v Hyder Akbar>这其实是一个非常暖心的故事。我其实很兴奋能回来。我有点意识到，我想，好吧，也许好消息并不总是那么有趣报道。我想，但最终回来会很令人兴奋。我非常有希望，感觉相当愉快。

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:26.740
<v Ira Glass>Because it was so hopeful that the Afghan National Army was doing a decent job?
<v Ira Glass>因为阿富汗国民军做得不错，所以很有希望？

00:25:26.740 --> 00:25:44.270
<v Hyder Akbar>Exactly. Exactly, that there's hope for this country, there's hope for security in this country, there's hope for stability in this country. And as somebody who has spent the last 10 years involved in this place, that was incredible news for me.
<v Hyder Akbar>正是。正是，这个国家有希望，这个国家的安全有希望，这个国家的稳定有希望。作为过去 10 年一直参与这个地方的人，这对我是不可思议的好消息。

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:59.890
<v Ira Glass>Hyder Akbar. He's the author of  the book Come Back to Afghanistan. This song that's playing right now is one that he swears was playing on his iPod when he was ambushed. And it's such a weird choice that it's hard to believe that he'd make that up. It's the Geto Boys' 1991 hit, "Mind Playing Tricks On Me."
<v Ira Glass>Hyder Akbar。他是《回到阿富汗》一书的作者。现在播放的这首歌，他发誓在他遭到伏击时，他的 iPod 里正在播放这首歌。这是一个如此奇怪的选择，以至于很难相信他是编出来的。这是 Geto Boys 1991 年的热门歌曲《Mind Playing Tricks On Me》。

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:20.890
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "MIND PLAYING TRICKS ON ME" BY GETO BOYS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - GETO BOYS 的《MIND PLAYING TRICKS ON ME》]

00:26:20.890 --> 00:26:31.370
<v Ira Glass>Well, if you live in a part of the country that has already had snow this year, you may find it hard to relate to the thrill experienced by some people in Arizona this past week. Lisa Pollak explains.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，如果你住在今年已经下过雪的地区，你可能很难理解过去一周亚利桑那州一些人经历的那种兴奋。Lisa Pollak 解释道。

00:26:31.370 --> 00:27:01.560
<v Lisa Pollak>When I heard that an outdoor mall in Tuscon was promising snow on Saturday, a day when the high temperature hit 77 degrees, I figured there were only two possible explanations-- a pre-Christmas meteorological miracle or a ski resort style snow machine. But there was, in fact, a third option. I heard about it from Melanie Sutton, the senior marketing manager at La Encantada mall, the mall that sponsors the annual event known as the Enchanted Snowfall. She said that what we were about to see falling from sky that night was a desert style snow.
<v Lisa Pollak>当我听说图森的一家户外购物中心承诺周六会有雪时——那天最高气温达到 77 华氏度（约 25 摄氏度）——我想只有两种可能的解释——一场圣诞前的气象奇迹，或者滑雪场式的造雪机。但实际上，还有第三种选择。我是从 Melanie Sutton 那里听说的，她是 La Encantada 购物中心的资深营销经理，该商场赞助了被称为“迷人降雪”的年度活动。她说那天晚上我们从天空中看到的飘落物是一种沙漠式的雪。

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:05.640
<v Melanie Sutton>As our tagline, we say, "98% magic, 2% soap."
<v Melanie Sutton>我们的口号是，“98% 的魔法，2% 的肥皂”。

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:11.540
<v Lisa Pollak>Oh, wow. So it literally is, like, that's the ingredient, is soap? I mean, besides the magic.
<v Lisa Pollak>哦，哇。所以它字面上就是，那就是成分，肥皂？我是说，除了魔法之外。

00:27:11.540 --> 00:27:17.190
<v Melanie Sutton>Correct. The ingredient is soap. But they're shaped as snowflakes.
<v Melanie Sutton>正确。成分是肥皂。但它们被塑造成雪花的形状。

00:27:17.190 --> 00:27:25.800
<v Lisa Pollak>Got that? Me, neither. But minutes later, along with hundreds of parents and kids in the mall's open-air courtyard, I had a chance to see it for myself.
<v Lisa Pollak>明白了吗？我也没有。但几分钟后，和商场露天庭院里的数百名父母和孩子一起，我有机会亲眼看到了。

00:27:25.800 --> 00:27:26.910
<v Dad 1>Oh, oh, here we go.
<v Dad 1>哦，哦，我们开始吧。

00:27:26.910 --> 00:27:32.900
<v Lisa Pollak>"Oh, oh, here we go," I heard one of the dads say. Then there was a whooshing sound, followed by white stuff falling from the sky.
<v Lisa Pollak>“哦，哦，我们要开始了，”我听到一位爸爸说。然后传来一阵呼啸声，接着白色的东西从天而降。

00:27:32.900 --> 00:27:38.390
<v Child 1>It's snowing!
<v Child 1>下雪了！

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:53.190
<v Lisa Pollak>From a distance, I can report that it did indeed resemble snow-- heavy, white flakes lit up by spotlights in the darkness. Up close, though, it was hard to be fooled. Imagine little clumps of bath suds or globs of latte foam, with a faintly fresh smell.
<v Lisa Pollak>从远处看，我可以报告它确实很像雪——在聚光灯照射下，黑暗中重重的白色雪花。近距离看，却很难被骗过。想象一下一小团沐浴露或拿铁泡沫，带有一种淡淡的清新气味。

00:27:53.190 --> 00:27:54.650
<v Dad 2>There it is, look!
<v Dad 2>就在那儿，看！

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:59.700
<v Lisa Pollak>This man is talking to his three-year-old son, Cameron, who at first seemed to be saying how much he liked the snow--
<v Lisa Pollak>这位男士正在和三岁的儿子 Cameron 说话，他起初看起来好像在说他多么喜欢这场雪——

00:27:59.700 --> 00:28:01.160
<v Cameron>I don't like snow.
<v Cameron>我不喜欢雪。

00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:02.630
<v Dad 2>You like snow.
<v Dad 2>你喜欢雪。

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:06.730
<v Lisa Pollak>--but on closer examination, was actually saying how much he didn't like the snow.
<v Lisa Pollak>——但仔细观察，他其实是在说他有多么不喜欢这场雪。

00:28:06.730 --> 00:28:08.210
<v Cameron>Don't like snow.
<v Cameron>不喜欢雪。

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:09.200
<v Dad 2>It's OK.
<v Dad 2>没事的。

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:11.180
<v Cameron>I don't like snow.
<v Cameron>我不喜欢雪。

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:13.170
<v Dad 2>It's OK.
<v Dad 2>没事的。

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:31.800
<v Lisa Pollak>Cameron was the exception. All around me, kids seemed to be going insane with joy. Over by the Apple store, a group of them had planted themselves under one of the faux snow machines and seemed to be trying to get as much white foam on their hair and clothes as possible. Others ran around with outstretched hands, trying to catch enough to make mushy snowballs and smash it on each other's heads.
<v Lisa Pollak>Cameron 是个例外。我周围的孩子们似乎兴奋得要发疯了。在苹果商店旁边，一群人把自己安放在其中一台仿造雪机下，似乎试图让尽可能多的白色泡沫落在他们的头发和衣服上。其他人张开双手跑来跑去，试图接住足够的泡沫来制作糊状的雪球，然后砸在彼此头上。

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:32.710
<v Lisa Pollak>[CHILD LAUGHING]
<v Lisa Pollak>[儿童笑声]

00:28:32.710 --> 00:28:34.980
<v Child 2>My hair!
<v Child 2>我的头发！

00:28:34.980 --> 00:28:40.080
<v Lisa Pollak>One boy walked up to me with a mass of suds in each hand and a look on his face like he couldn't believe his good fortune.
<v Lisa Pollak>一个小男孩走过来，两只手里拿着一大团泡沫，脸上带着一种难以置信的幸运表情。

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:42.750
<v Boy 1>Look at how much snow I have.
<v Boy 1>看看我有多少雪。

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:48.910
<v Lisa Pollak>Suddenly I felt a sense of duty. Shouldn't these kids know the truth? I would break it to them gently.
<v Lisa Pollak>突然我感到一种责任感。难道这些孩子不应该知道真相吗？我会温柔地告诉他们。

00:28:48.910 --> 00:28:50.850
<v Lisa Pollak>It looks kind of like soap.
<v Lisa Pollak>它看起来有点像肥皂。

00:28:50.850 --> 00:29:04.350
<v Boy 1>That's because it is soap. It's kind of like regular snow. But if it gets in your eyes, your eyes just terribly burn. It's just bubbles, but I can't believe it! I've never seen this happen before!
<v Boy 1>那是因为它就是肥皂。它有点像普通的雪。但如果它进了你的眼睛，你的眼睛就会剧烈刺痛。它只是气泡，但我不敢相信！我从来没见过这种事发生！

00:29:04.350 --> 00:29:07.280
<v Child 3>So fun! And it's soap!
<v Child 3>太有趣了！而且它是肥皂！

00:29:07.280 --> 00:29:16.530
<v Boy 2>I tasted it. It tastes like soap.
<v Boy 2>我尝了一下。尝起来像肥皂。

00:29:16.530 --> 00:29:25.950
<v Lisa Pollak>About halfway through the show, I noticed I was starting to cough. And I wasn't the only one. I overheard this lady talking to her husband. Everyone's coughing, she said.
<v Lisa Pollak>节目进行到一半，我注意到我开始咳嗽了。我不是唯一一个。我无意中听到这位女士和她丈夫说话。每个人都在咳嗽，她说。

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:28.070
<v Man 4>We're all going to get cancer.
<v Man 4>我们都会得癌症的。

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:28.900
<v Lisa Pollak>What did you say?
<v Lisa Pollak>你说什么？

00:29:28.900 --> 00:29:30.380
<v Man 4>We're all going to get cancer.
<v Man 4>我们都会得癌症的。

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:32.310
<v Lisa Pollak>Why did you say-- [COUGHS]
<v Lisa Pollak>你为什么说——[咳嗽声]

00:29:32.310 --> 00:29:36.530
<v Man 4>Just listen. Everybody's standing here coughing, right? So it can't be good for you.
<v Man 4>听着。每个人都站在这里咳嗽，对吧？所以这对你肯定没好处。

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:37.420
<v Lisa Pollak>[COUGHS]
<v Lisa Pollak>[咳嗽声]

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:40.000
<v Man 4>Yeah. Right? There you go. There you go. Get you some.
<v Man 4>是的。对吧？就是这样。就是这样。你也来点。

00:29:40.000 --> 00:30:03.790
<v Lisa Pollak>I checked with Melanie, the mall's marketing manager. She assured me that whatever was in the bubbles had been tested and was nontoxic. Though I believed her, I was a little relieved when the Enchanted Snowfall powered down after 15 minutes. Like a lot of things about the holidays, it was at its best from a distance, and a little went a long way.
<v Lisa Pollak>我向商场的营销经理 Melanie 确认了。她向我保证，气泡里的任何成分都经过测试且无毒。尽管我相信她，但当 15 分钟后“迷人降雪”停止时，我还是感到有点解脱。就像假期里的很多事情一样，它在远距离看时最好，少一点反而更有趣。

00:30:03.790 --> 00:30:20.120
<v Ira Glass>Lisa Pollak. Coming up, a guy walks into a bar-- usually not a life-or-death situation. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>Lisa Pollak。接下来，一个人走进酒吧——这通常不是生死攸关的情况。那将在来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际的节目中，稍后回来继续。

00:30:20.120 --> 00:30:31.540
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. This week, instead of the usual sort of theme we do on our show, our theme is just the seven days that have just passed. We have stories, small and personal, and big and national, and international.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是 Ira Glass。本周，我们没有像往常那样做任何主题，我们的主题就是刚刚过去的七天。我们有小而私人的故事，有宏大而全国性的，还有国际性的故事。

00:30:31.540 --> 00:30:49.200
<v Ira Glass>Monday, you probably heard, House Speaker John Boehner issued his counterproposal to avoid the fiscal cliff. It was promptly rejected by the White House. By Wednesday, there were front-page stories that Republican leaders are considering a fallback plan to extend tax cuts to the middle class now, fight about the rest of it in January or February.
<v Ira Glass>周一，你可能听说过，众议院议长 John Boehner 发表了他的反提案以避免财政悬崖。该方案立即被白宫否决。到了周三，头版故事说共和党领导人正在考虑一个后备计划，即现在延长中产阶级的减税政策，在一月或二月再为剩下的部分进行争论。

00:30:49.200 --> 00:31:08.490
<v Ira Glass>Also this week, Boehner cracked the whip in his own party. Republicans who didn't vote his way lost committee assignments. He warned to other House members that he would be watching. To put all of this in perspective, I called veteran Congress-watcher Norman Ornstein, who happened to be in Prague when I phoned him. His official title is Resident Scholar at the American Enterprise Institute.
<v Ira Glass>同样在本周，Boehner 在他自己的党内挥舞鞭子。没有按他方式投票的共和党人失去了委员会职务。他警告其他众议院成员，他会看着他们的。为了全面了解这一切，我打电话给资深国会观察员 Norman Ornstein，我打电话时他恰好在布拉格。他的正式头衔是美国企业研究所的常驻学者。

00:31:08.490 --> 00:31:11.030
<v Norman Ornstein>But all I care about is that you mention the book--
<v Norman Ornstein>但我只关心你提到那本书——

00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:12.310
<v Ira Glass>And the title of the book is?
<v Ira Glass>那本书的标题是？

00:31:12.310 --> 00:31:20.630
<v Norman Ornstein>--which is, It's Even Worse Than It Looks-- How the American Constitutional System Collided with the New Politics of Extremism.
<v Norman Ornstein>——也就是，《情况比看起来更糟——美国宪法体系如何与极端主义的新政治相碰撞》。

00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:22.010
<v Ira Glass>Consider it mentioned.
<v Ira Glass>算提到过了。

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:23.020
<v Norman Ornstein>OK.
<v Norman Ornstein>好的。

00:31:23.020 --> 00:31:28.390
<v Ira Glass>So you're in Prague this week. Isn't this kind of a big week not to be near Capitol Hill for somebody like you?
<v Ira Glass>所以你这周在布拉格。对于像你这样的人来说，这周难道不是不待在国会山附近太重要了吗？

00:31:28.390 --> 00:31:32.040
<v Norman Ornstein>It's a good week to be away from Capitol Hill in a lot of ways.
<v Norman Ornstein>在很多方面，这确实是个离开国会山的好日子。

00:31:32.040 --> 00:31:33.400
<v Ira Glass>How so?
<v Ira Glass>怎么说？

00:31:33.400 --> 00:32:01.510
<v Norman Ornstein>Well, it seems like a big week. But the fact is we're in the middle of an end game. And I've been kind of bemused by all of the focus on the every-minute maneuvering of Speaker John Boehner and President Obama, including a heavy focus on the fact that Boehner didn't pose for a picture with the President at the White House Christmas party.
<v Norman Ornstein>嗯，这看起来好像是重要的一周。但事实是，我们正处于终局博弈之中。我对人们过于关注众议院议长 John Boehner 和奥巴马总统的每一分钟博弈感到好笑，包括重点关注 Boehner 没有在白宫圣诞派对上与总统合影。

00:32:01.510 --> 00:32:11.160
<v Norman Ornstein>And there's one important principle to keep in mind. When does an end game end? At the end. And the end is not this week.
<v Norman Ornstein>记住一个重要的原则。终局博弈什么时候结束？在最后。而现在并不是最后。

00:32:11.160 --> 00:32:15.730
<v Ira Glass>Are you basically saying that this is a week we might as well ignore the news from Capitol Hill?
<v Ira Glass>你是说这周我们最好忽略国会山的新闻吗？

00:32:15.730 --> 00:32:25.050
<v Norman Ornstein>Yes. I'm saying that there are naturally going to be people who follow every little twist and turn. But it doesn't really matter.
<v Norman Ornstein>是的。我是说自然会有关注每一个微小曲折的人。但这并不重要。

00:32:25.050 --> 00:32:32.020
<v Ira Glass>So this is the thing that I was wondering, is, when you look at this maneuvering, can you tell how it's going to play out?
<v Ira Glass>这就是我好奇的地方，当你观察这种博弈时，你能看出它会如何发展吗？

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:50.960
<v Norman Ornstein>The answer is two-fold. The first part is, no, you can't tell how it's going to play out. But all of these ploys and, in some ways, histrionics are par for the course. So taking the temperature on any one of them doesn't really tell us that much.
<v Norman Ornstein>答案有两个方面。第一部分是，不，你看不出它会如何发展。但所有这些策略，某种程度上甚至是做戏，都是家常便饭。所以仅仅关注其中任何一个并没有告诉我们太多。

00:32:50.960 --> 00:33:11.890
<v Norman Ornstein>Now, what I would have to say, though, is the second point. And it's frankly a giant caveat here. We are going through the most dysfunctional politics I've seen in 43 years. So I think there's a pretty good chance we'll get a patchwork deal to get us past this dilemma before the end of the year.
<v Norman Ornstein>现在，我不得不说的是第二点。坦率地说，这里有一个巨大的告诫。我们正在经历我 43 年来见过最功能失调的政治。所以我认为我们很有可能在今年年底前达成一项拼凑协议来摆脱这个困境。

00:33:11.890 --> 00:33:33.030
<v Norman Ornstein>But I also think that there's a substantial chance, much higher than it would have been if we were looking at the process 20 years ago or 10 years ago, even 5 years ago, that we'll slip over into January. And it might take a jolt, a serious jolt, from the markets to bring us to some kind of conclusion here.
<v Norman Ornstein>但我也认为有很大的可能性，比我们 20 年前、10 年前甚至 5 年前看待这个过程时要高得多，我们会拖到一月。这可能需要一次冲击，来自市场的严重冲击，才能让我们在这里达成某种结论。

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:42.760
<v Ira Glass>Norman Ornstein. I asked him, why do they even bother going through the theater this week? Why not just skip to the part where they actually hammer out the deal? His answer, I thought, was interesting.
<v Ira Glass>Norman Ornstein。我问他，他们为什么还要费心在本周做戏？为什么不直接跳到他们真正敲定协议的那一部分？我觉得他的回答很有趣。

00:33:42.760 --> 00:34:08.540
<v Ira Glass>He said that the Republicans need to demonstrate to Grover Norquist and Rush Limbaugh and all their own constituents that they tried everything, their back is against the wall, there was no other choice before they can raise taxes. The Democrats have their own version of it. They have to demonstrate to their base they had no choice but to cut entitlements before any deal. Both sides need to say no, he says, for a while.
<v Ira Glass>他说共和党人需要向 Grover Norquist、Rush Limbaugh 和他们自己所有的选民证明，在提高税收之前，他们尝试了一切，背水一战，别无选择。民主党人也有他们自己的版本。他们必须向他们的基本盘证明，在达成任何协议之前，他们除了削减福利别无选择。双方都需要说不，他说，持续一段时间。

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:19.320
<v Ira Glass>Wednesday night, New Orleans, French Quarter. A 25-year-old blonde girl named Joni Cooper is going up to everybody in this bar, asking a question that you probably wouldn't expect to hear on a night out drinking.
<v Ira Glass>周三晚上，新奥尔良，法国区。一个名叫 Joni Cooper 的 25 岁金发女孩走到这家酒吧的每个人面前，问了一个你可能没料到会在酒吧喝酒之夜听到的问题。

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:20.520
<v Joni Cooper>Hey, guys.
<v Joni Cooper>嘿，伙计们。

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:20.960
<v Bar Patron 1>How are you?
<v Bar Patron 1>你们好吗？

00:34:20.960 --> 00:34:22.210
<v Joni Cooper>Can I come chat with you?
<v Joni Cooper>我能和你们聊聊吗？

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:22.960
<v Bar Patron 1>Sure.
<v Bar Patron 1>当然。

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:30.980
<v Joni Cooper>Well, my name's Joni. And I'm here with NO/AIDS. We're doing free HIV testing today. Would y'all maybe be interested in getting tested tonight?
<v Joni Cooper>嗯，我叫 Joni。我代表 NO/AIDS 组织。我们今天在做免费 HIV 检测。你们今晚可能有兴趣检测一下吗？

00:34:30.980 --> 00:35:01.130
<v Ira Glass>NO/AIDS is an organization in Louisiana that works to prevent and reduce the spread of HIV and help treat people who are infected. Louisiana has the fourth highest estimated AIDS rate in the country. In 2011, nearly 60% of new HIV diagnoses in Louisiana were gay man. 74% were African Americans. So NO/AIDS tries to make finding at your HIV status as convenient as they possibly can, which means, for them, going into bars, asking people to put down their drink for 15 minutes, and come get tested. They get the results on the spot.
<v Ira Glass>NO/AIDS 是路易斯安那州的一个组织，致力于预防和减少艾滋病毒的传播，并帮助治疗感染者。路易斯安那州的艾滋病发病率估计在全国排名第四。2011 年，路易斯安那州近 60% 的艾滋病毒新诊断病例是男同性恋者。74% 是非裔美国人。所以 NO/AIDS 试图尽可能方便地让人们了解他们的艾滋病毒状况，这意味着对他们来说，要走进酒吧，请求人们放下手中的饮料 15 分钟，来接受检测。他们当场就能得到结果。

00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:02.570
<v Bar Patron 2>It's my birthday, so--
<v Bar Patron 2>今天是我的生日，所以——

00:35:02.570 --> 00:35:05.240
<v Joni Cooper>[SCREAMS]. Happy birthday!
<v Joni Cooper>[尖叫声]。生日快乐！

00:35:05.240 --> 00:35:12.880
<v Ira Glass>Joni is pitching a group of guys who are out for their friend's 30th birthday. You might think this would not be a receptive crowd, but listen to what happens.
<v Ira Glass>Joni 正在游说一群出来为朋友庆祝 30 岁生日的男士。你可能认为这不会是一个乐于接受的群体，但听听发生了什么。

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:15.160
<v Bar Patron 2>You're doing it right now? Like, what time?
<v Bar Patron 2>你们现在就做？比如说，什么时候？

00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:16.530
<v Joni Cooper>We're doing it right now.
<v Joni Cooper>我们现在就做。

00:35:16.530 --> 00:35:17.050
<v Bar Patron 2>Let's do it.
<v Bar Patron 2>我们做吧。

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:17.670
<v Joni Cooper>You want to go?
<v Joni Cooper>你想去吗？

00:35:17.670 --> 00:35:18.320
<v Bar Patron 2>Let's go.
<v Bar Patron 2>我们走吧。

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:18.730
<v Joni Cooper>All right!
<v Joni Cooper>好的！

00:35:18.730 --> 00:35:20.850
<v Bar Patron 2>Let's go. I'm going to get tested. You coming?
<v Bar Patron 2>走吧。我要去检测。你来吗？

00:35:20.850 --> 00:35:21.330
<v Bar Patron 3>Where?
<v Bar Patron 3>在哪？

00:35:21.330 --> 00:35:23.240
<v Bar Patron 2>Right here, right now.
<v Bar Patron 2>就在这儿，就在现在。

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:31.740
<v Robin Pearce>You catch someone by surprise. You kind of catch someone off guard, and they're like, oh, well, I guess it's here. I've never done it.
<v Robin Pearce>你让某人感到惊讶。你有点让他们措手不及，他们会想，哦，好吧，我想是在这里吧。我从来没做过。

00:35:31.740 --> 00:35:38.040
<v Ira Glass>Robin Pearce runs the testing program, which has testing sites all over New Orleans, not just in bars.
<v Ira Glass>Robin Pearce 负责这项检测计划，在整个新奥尔良都有检测点，不仅在酒吧。

00:35:38.040 --> 00:35:51.240
<v Robin Pearce>The statistic is that 1.2 million people in the United States are living with HIV, and one in five of them don't know they have it. So finding the people who don't know that they have HIV is the point of what we're doing.
<v Robin Pearce>统计数据是，美国有 120 万人携带艾滋病毒，其中五分之一的人不知道自己携带病毒。所以寻找不知道自己感染了艾滋病毒的人就是我们所做工作的重点。

00:35:51.240 --> 00:36:20.600
<v Ira Glass>The week that we're covering here today happened to begin with World AIDS Day. And thinking about that, we sent out a writer, Nathaniel Rich, to watch the HIV testing at one of the highest risk venues NO/AIDS tests at-- Club New Orleans, a five-story sex club for men in the center of the French Quarter, which means that this story might not be appropriate for all children or maybe any of them. It acknowledges the existence of sex. Here's Nathaniel. We changed the name of the guy he's writing about in this story.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们要报道的这一周恰好以世界艾滋病日开始。考虑到这一点，我们派出一名作家 Nathaniel Rich，去观察 NO/AIDS 进行 HIV 检测的最高风险场所之一——新奥尔良俱乐部（Club New Orleans），这是一个位于法国区中心地带的五层楼男子性俱乐部，这意味着这个故事可能不适合所有儿童，甚至可能不适合任何人。它承认了性行为的存在。这是 Nathaniel 的报道。我们更改了他在这个故事中提到的那个人的名字。

00:36:20.600 --> 00:36:41.090
<v Nathaniel Rich>Room 300 was the largest bedroom in the club, just large enough to fit a chair and a queen size bed covered with red satin sheets. Sitting cross-legged on the bed was Alex Lee, a 25-year-old counselor for NO/AIDS, who's also a student at the University of New Orleans. He's majoring in anthropology. In fact, he had a final exam the next morning.
<v Nathaniel Rich>300 号房间是俱乐部里最大的卧室，刚好够放一把椅子和一张铺着红色缎面床单的大床。盘腿坐在床上的是 Alex Lee，一名 25 岁的 NO/AIDS 顾问，同时也是新奥尔良大学的学生。他主修人类学。事实上，他第二天早上还有一场期末考试。

00:36:41.090 --> 00:37:03.380
<v Nathaniel Rich>When Mark entered the room, Alex was reading an essay about Occam's razor. Alex asked Mark to sit in the chair. He explained that the HIV test would only detect an infection that occurred more than three months ago. He showed Mark how to use an applicator to swab this mouth-- first his upper gums, then his lower gums. When Mark was finished, he dipped the end of the applicator into a small vial.
<v Nathaniel Rich>当 Mark 进入房间时，Alex 正在读一篇关于“奥卡姆剃刀”的文章。Alex 请 Mark 坐在椅子上。他解释说 HIV 检测只能检测到发生在三个多月前的感染。他向 Mark 展示了如何使用棉签擦拭口腔——先是上牙龈，然后是下牙龈。当 Mark 完成后，他将棉签末端浸入一个小瓶子中。

00:37:03.380 --> 00:37:19.960
<v Nathaniel Rich>Alex set a timer to 20 minutes, the time it takes for a test result to appear, and placed it on the bedside table between them. He asked Mark a series of questions. "When was your last test?" "About a year ago," said Mark, "at a bar, in fact."
<v Nathaniel Rich>Alex 将计时器定为 20 分钟，这是检测结果出现所需的时间，并把它放在他们之间的床头柜上。他问了 Mark 一系列问题。“你上次检测是什么时候？”“大约一年前，”Mark 说，“事实上是在一家酒吧。”

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:39.210
<v Nathaniel Rich>"In the past 12 months," asked Alex, "have you had anal sex with a man?" "Yes," said Mark. "Have you had sex without using a condom?" "As a giver," said Mark. "How about sex with an anonymous, casual, or internet partner, someone you didn't know too well?" "I'm sure I have," said Mark.
<v Nathaniel Rich>“在过去 12 个月里，”Alex 问，“你有没有和男性发生过肛交？”“是的，”Mark 说。“你有没有在不使用避孕套的情况下发生性关系？”“作为主动方，”Mark 说。“有没有和匿名的、随意的或互联网伴侣发生性关系，那种你不太了解的人？”“我肯定有过，”Mark 说。

00:37:39.210 --> 00:37:52.810
<v Nathaniel Rich>He seemed to become increasingly anxious. He crossed his arms, holding his elbows, hugging his chest. "Have you knowingly had sex with someone who's living with HIV?" "No." "Have you injected any drugs?" "No."
<v Nathaniel Rich>他看起来越来越焦虑。他交叉双臂，紧紧抱着胸口。“你是否明知对方患有艾滋病毒还与其发生性关系？”“没有。”“你注射过毒品吗？”“没有。”

00:37:52.810 --> 00:38:13.600
<v Nathaniel Rich>"Have you had sex with someone who injects drugs?" Mark paused for two seconds. "No," he said. Alex started the next question, but Mark interrupted him. "Yes," he said, "yes, I have." "Sex with someone who injects drugs?" "Yeah. I just didn't know it until after."
<v Nathaniel Rich>“你有没有和注射过毒品的人发生性关系？”Mark 停顿了两秒钟。“没有，”他说。Alex 开始问下一个问题，但 Mark 打断了他。“是的，”他说，“是的，我有。”“和注射毒品的人发生性关系？”“是的。我直到事后才知道。”

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:24.020
<v Nathaniel Rich>The timer beeped. "Does that mean it's ready?" asked Mark. "No," said Alex. "It just beeps sometimes."
<v Nathaniel Rich>计时器发出哔哔声。“那意味着准备好了吗？”Mark 问。“不，”Alex 说。“有时候它就是会响。”

00:38:24.020 --> 00:38:42.050
<v Nathaniel Rich>"It's so funny," said Mark, "and this is so crude, but I did have intercourse last night, not wearing a condom. It's just weird. The guy told me he was negative, but for some reason, after all was said and done, I thought he was really positive. And then I was thinking on the walk home how stupid that was."
<v Nathaniel Rich>“这太搞笑了，”Mark 说，“这很粗俗，但我确实昨晚发生了性行为，没戴避孕套。这太奇怪了。那个人告诉我他是阴性，但出于某种原因，一切结束后，我以为他是阳性。然后我在走回家的路上心想这太蠢了。”

00:38:42.050 --> 00:39:06.600
<v Nathaniel Rich>Mark had been busy all day at work, so it had been easy to avoid thinking about it. But that evening, he went for a walk and soon found himself standing in front of the club. It was odd, Mark said, because he hadn't visited the club for a year. As soon as he stepped inside, a voice came on the loudspeaker announcing that there would be free HIV testing that evening. Suddenly, Mark understood why he had come to the club, and he felt scared.
<v Nathaniel Rich>Mark 整天在工作中很忙，所以很容易不去想这件事。但那天晚上，他去散步，很快发现自己站在俱乐部前面。这很奇怪，Mark 说，因为他已经有一年没去过那个俱乐部了。他一走进去，扬声器里就传来一个声音，宣布当晚有免费的 HIV 检测。突然，Mark 明白了自己为什么来到俱乐部，他感到很害怕。

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:28.590
<v Nathaniel Rich>There were five minutes left on the timer. "I'm really anxious," said Mark. He looked it. He was jittery. He rose from his chair. He said he was going to walk around and come back when his result was ready.
<v Nathaniel Rich>计时器还剩五分钟。“我真的很焦虑，”Mark 说。他看起来就是那样。他很焦躁。他从椅子上站起来。他说他要到处走走，等结果出来后再回来。

00:39:28.590 --> 00:39:43.840
<v Nathaniel Rich>But five minutes later, when the timer went off, Mark was gone. Alex waited another 10 minutes, then another 10. A different man was tested, waited for his result, then left. Still no sign of Mark.
<v Nathaniel Rich>但五分钟后，当计时器响起时，Mark 已经不见了。Alex 又等了 10 分钟，接着又是 10 分钟。另一个男人接受了检测，等待结果，然后离开了。Mark 仍然没影。

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:58.050
<v Nathaniel Rich>Alex was mystified. In his two years testing men at bars, nobody had ever fled before finding out his results. It was now almost 11:30. His shift had official ended half an hour ago, and his anthropology final was at 10:00 AM.
<v Nathaniel Rich>Alex 很困惑。在他为酒吧男士检测的两年里，从来没有人会在知道结果之前逃跑。现在快 11:30 了。他的轮班半小时前正式结束了，而他的人类学期末考试是在早上 10:00。

00:39:58.050 --> 00:40:11.570
<v Nathaniel Rich>As Alex gathered his testing materials, his colleague, Brandon, decided to make one final effort to find Mark. Brandon started in the gym. Mark wasn't there. Nor was Mark in the sauna or the whirlpool.
<v Nathaniel Rich>当 Alex 收拾检测材料时，他的同事 Brandon 决定做最后一次尝试来寻找 Mark。Brandon 从健身房开始找。Mark 不在那里。Mark 也不在桑拿房或漩涡浴缸里。

00:40:11.570 --> 00:40:23.140
<v Nathaniel Rich>He was not in the room called the Bookstore, which doesn't have any books, nor was he in the Barracks. He was not in the so-called Porno Room, in which every surface is made of black rubber. And he was not in the Dungeon.
<v Nathaniel Rich>他不在名为“书店”的房间里，里面根本没有书，他也不在“军营”里。他不在所谓的“色情房”里，那里的每个表面都是用黑色橡胶制成的。他也不在“地牢”里。

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:34.000
<v Nathaniel Rich>Brandon paced through the narrow hallways, which are lined with more than 50 tiny bedrooms. Some of the doors were ajar. He saw harnesses and swings. He saw men watching videos.
<v Nathaniel Rich>Brandon 在狭窄的走廊里踱步，那里排列着 50 多个小卧室。有些门半开着。他看到了吊具和秋千。他看到男人们在看视频。

00:40:34.000 --> 00:40:48.470
<v Nathaniel Rich>And then, in another bedroom, Brandon saw Mark. He was sitting on a bed, talking with a naked man. "Oh," said Mark, "are you looking for me?"
<v Nathaniel Rich>然后，在另一个卧室里，Brandon 看到了 Mark。他正坐在床上，和一个裸体男人交谈。“哦，”Mark 说，“你在找我吗？”

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:58.190
<v Nathaniel Rich>Five minutes later, Mark returned to room 300. His fingers were trembling. He was too nervous to sit. Alex told him that his test had come back negative.
<v Nathaniel Rich>五分钟后，Mark 回到了 300 号房间。他的手指在颤抖。他太紧张了，不敢坐下。Alex 告诉他检测结果是阴性。

00:40:58.190 --> 00:41:08.350
<v Nathaniel Rich>"Yay!" Mark shouted. He was ecstatic. He asked Alex to show him the result on the test form. "God, I'm so happy. Thank you. Oh my god."
<v Nathaniel Rich>“太好了！”Mark 大喊。他欣喜若狂。他让 Alex 在检测表格上向他展示结果。“上帝，我太高兴了。谢谢你。噢天哪。”

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:33.090
<v Nathaniel Rich>He thanked Alex a few more times and said he was grateful for the work that NO/AIIDS was doing. Mark even said he wanted to volunteer for the organization, that he felt, as a member of the New Orleans gay community, it was the right thing to do. Then, negative test results in hand, he disappeared into the club.
<v Nathaniel Rich>他又感谢了 Alex 几次，并说他很感激 NO/AIDS 所做的工作。Mark 甚至说他想为该组织做志愿者，他觉得，作为新奥尔良同性恋社区的一员，这是正确的事情。然后，拿着阴性检测结果，他消失在俱乐部里。

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:36.230
<v Ira Glass>Nathaniel Rich. He's the author of the forthcoming book Odds Against Tomorrow.
<v Ira Glass>Nathaniel Rich。他是即将出版的书《胜算》（Odds Against Tomorrow）的作者。

00:41:36.230 --> 00:41:46.530
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "ERASE" BY THE CHAINSMOKERS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - THE CHAINSMOKERS 的《ERASE》]

00:41:46.530 --> 00:42:10.730
<v Ira Glass>This, what you're listening to right now, is a Samantha Ronson remix, released this week by the Chainsmokers. Except for the Geto Boys, all the songs between stories today was a release or on albums that were released in the last seven days.
<v Ira Glass>这首你现在正在听的歌，是由 The Chainsmokers 本周发布的 Samantha Ronson 的混音作品。除了 Geto Boys 的歌，今天故事之间的所有歌曲都是新发行的，或者是在过去七天里发布的专辑里的歌。

00:42:10.730 --> 00:42:24.670
<v Ira Glass>A week can be a long time, when every day seems to shift the direction of your entire country. Egypt has had a week of demonstrations, counter demonstrations, and violence. The demonstrations are for and against the country's president, Mohammed Morsi.
<v Ira Glass>当每一天似乎都在改变整个国家的方向时，一周可能是一段很长的时间。埃及经历了一周的示威、反示威和暴力。示威活动是针对该国总统穆罕默德·穆尔西的支持者和反对者的。

00:42:24.670 --> 00:42:49.780
<v Ira Glass>And OK, quick review-- Morsi was democratically elected a couple months ago. But at the end of November, he shocked many Egyptians by issuing a decree giving himself enormous powers, basically putting all his decisions, past and present, above the law. He said he had to do this because judges appointed by Egypt's ousted dictator, Hosni Mubarak, were undermining him. Some of those judges had dissolved Egypt's newly elected parliament right before Morsi took office.
<v Ira Glass>好了，快速回顾一下——穆尔西是几个月前民主选举产生的。但在 11 月底，他发布了一项法令，赋予自己巨大的权力，基本将他过去和现在的所有决定置于法律之上，这让许多埃及人感到震惊。他说他必须这样做，因为被赶下台的独裁者胡斯尼·穆巴拉克任命的法官正在破坏他。其中一些法官在穆尔西上任前不久解散了埃及新当选的议会。

00:42:49.780 --> 00:43:06.850
<v Ira Glass>So now, if you follow the news at all, you've seen this-- both sides are dug in, each convinced that they represent true democracy in Egypt. There are a lot of ways for things to get worse, very few for them to get better. Nancy Updike reports on what has happened and how some of the anger is being spread around.
<v Ira Glass>所以现在，如果你关注新闻，你已经看到了这一点——双方都各执一词，每个人都确信他们代表了埃及真正的民主。事情恶化的方式有很多，变好的方式却很少。Nancy Updike 报道了发生了什么，以及一些愤怒是如何传播开来的。

00:43:06.850 --> 00:43:29.260
<v Nancy Updike>Up until this past Wednesday, opponents and supporters of President Morsi had held their rallies in different places-- on purpose, to avoid confrontations. But late in the day on Wednesday, supporters of President Morsi, members of the Muslim Brotherhood, marched to the presidential palace, where some anti-Morsi protesters were camped out. And after that, all night long, people fought or ran or hid.
<v Nancy Updike>直到上周三，总统穆尔西的反对者和支持者一直在不同的地方举行集会——这是故意的，为了避免对抗。但在周三晚些时候，总统穆尔西的支持者，穆斯林兄弟会的成员，游行到总统府，一些反穆尔西的抗议者在那里露营。此后，整夜，人们一直在战斗、奔跑或躲藏。

00:43:29.260 --> 00:43:47.020
<v Nancy Updike>By Thursday morning, seven people were dead, and hundreds were injured. And four of Morsi's advisers had resigned in protest. As of right now, Friday evening, around 6:00 PM Egypt time, the leaders of both sides aren't talking to each other, and protesters are still in the streets, separate and peaceful for the moment.
<v Nancy Updike>到周四早上，七人死亡，数百人受伤。穆尔西的四名顾问辞职以示抗议。截至目前，周五晚上，埃及时间下午 6:00 左右，双方领导人互不交谈，抗议者仍留在街头，目前暂时分开且和平。

00:43:47.020 --> 00:44:09.190
<v Nancy Updike>When I was in Cairo after Mubarak was ousted last year, I saw a scene that now seems impossible-- writers and editors, secular Egyptians, who were talking over croissants with members of a formerly violent Islamist group named Gamaa Islamiya, an ally of the Muslim Brotherhood. The secular men and the devout men weren't becoming friends. But they weren't beating each other up in the streets, either.
<v Nancy Updike>去年穆巴拉克被赶下台后，我在开罗时，看到了一个现在看起来不可能的场景——作家和编辑，世俗的埃及人，正在和以前一个暴力伊斯兰组织“伊斯兰集团”（Gamaa Islamiya）的成员，即穆斯林兄弟会的盟友，一起吃牛角面包。那些世俗的人和虔诚的人并没有成为朋友。但他们也没有在街上互殴。

00:44:09.190 --> 00:44:09.630
<v Nancy Updike>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Nancy Updike>[电话铃响]

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:10.950
<v Yusuf Al-qaid>Hello?
<v Yusuf Al-qaid>喂？

00:44:10.950 --> 00:44:12.510
<v Interpreter>[SPEAKING ARABIC].
<v Interpreter>[讲阿拉伯语]。

00:44:12.510 --> 00:44:31.120
<v Nancy Updike>On Thursday night, I made a phone call through an interpreter. She was shouting a bit to make herself heard. And we called one of the people from that meeting, an Egyptian novelist in his 70s named Yusuf al-Qaid to find out his take on the week's events. He remembered me, but he wanted to make absolutely sure I knew where he stands in this fight.
<v Nancy Updike>周四晚上，我通过翻译打了一个电话。她喊得有点响以便让我听到。我们给那次会议中的一个人打了电话，一位 70 多岁的埃及小说家 Yusuf al-Qaid，想了解他对本周事件的看法。他记得我，但他想百分之百确保我知道他在这场斗争中的立场。

00:44:31.120 --> 00:44:32.900
<v Yusuf Al-qaid>[SPEAKING ARABIC].
<v Yusuf Al-qaid>[讲阿拉伯语]。

00:44:32.900 --> 00:44:59.210
<v Translator>I would like our American journalist here to understand that I am not in any neutral position here. I am with a civilian modern state. I am against a religious state. I am against any rule based on religion.
<v Translator>我希望我们的美国记者了解，我在这里没有任何中立立场。我支持一个世俗的现代国家。我反对一个宗教国家。我反对任何基于宗教的统治。

00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:22.840
<v Nancy Updike>So against Morsi. And since he brought up the fact that I'm American, I told al-Qaid that I'd seen pictures of anti-Morsi protesters, people like him, carrying signs talking about the US and President Obama. I didn't mention this specific one to him, but I saw a sign that said, "Obama, your bitch is our dictator." The vehemence of al-Qaid's response caught me by surprise.
<v Nancy Updike>所以他反对穆尔西。既然他提到了我是美国人这一事实，我告诉 al-Qaid，我看到了反穆尔西抗议者的照片，像他这样的人，举着谈论美国和奥巴马总统的标语。我没有具体向他提及那一条，但我看到一个标语写着，“奥巴马，你的婊子是我们的独裁者。”Al-Qaid 回应的猛烈程度让我感到惊讶。

00:45:22.840 --> 00:46:11.200
<v Translator>In my view, the biggest betrayal that has taken place against the Egyptian people is the absolute support that the American administration has given to the Muslim Brotherhood. America is ignoring the violence that is conducted against the Egyptian people. America is completely silent and has voiced that its relationship with Egypt is strategic. And therefore, passing events do not affect this strategic relationship.
<v Translator>在我看来，对埃及人民最大的背叛是美国政府对穆斯林兄弟会的绝对支持。美国无视对埃及人民进行的暴力行为。美国保持完全沉默，并表示它与埃及的关系是战略性的。因此，发生的事件不影响这种战略关系。

00:46:11.200 --> 00:46:32.800
<v Nancy Updike>By strategic relationship, he means that the United States is more concerned about Egypt's dealings with Israel than about Egypt's dealings with its own citizens. This week, more than ever, I realized that no matter how angry and caught up in their own national issues a person is, depending on what country they're in, they probably still have plenty of anger left over for the United States.
<v Nancy Updike>所谓战略关系，他的意思是美国更关心埃及与以色列的交易，而不是埃及与本国公民的交易。本周，我比以往任何时候都更意识到，无论一个人多么愤怒并沉浸在他们自己的国家事务中，取决于他们所在的国家，他们可能仍然会对美国怀有充足的余怒。

00:46:32.800 --> 00:46:55.830
<v Nancy Updike>After I hung up with al-Qaid, I called another person I met in Cairo last year-- completely different guy, a religious man, young, 28 years old, named Mohammed Agiba. Mohammed spent years in the Muslim Brotherhood. But now he's marching against Morsi too. He knew Morsi's power grab would divide the country. And he thinks the proposed constitution is a disaster that has to be stopped.
<v Nancy Updike>在和 al-Qaid 通完话后，我给去年我在开罗遇到的另一个人打了电话——一个完全不同的人，一个虔诚的人，年轻，28 岁，名叫 Mohammed Agiba。Mohammed 在穆斯林兄弟会待了多年。但现在他也加入了反穆尔西的游行。他知道穆尔西攫取权力会分裂这个国家。他认为提议的宪法是一场必须阻止的灾难。

00:46:55.830 --> 00:47:19.310
<v Nancy Updike>And talking about the constitution is when we ended up at the same place I ended up with al-Qaid, the secular guy. Mohammed talked about how the constitution calls for a joint civilian military council, weighted toward the military, that has to be consulted before the president can declare a war. The council also has a role in deciding on the military's budget. Mohammed sees the council as one thing only.
<v Nancy Updike>谈到宪法时，我们最终得出了我和世俗人士 al-Qaid 相同的结论。Mohammed 谈到宪法如何呼吁建立一个偏向军方的联合民事军事委员会，在总统宣布战争前必须咨询该委员会。该委员会在决定军费预算方面也发挥作用。Mohammed 认为这个委员会只有一种功能。

00:47:19.310 --> 00:47:20.670
<v Mohammed Agiba>[SPEAKING ARABIC].
<v Mohammed Agiba>[讲阿拉伯语]。

00:47:20.670 --> 00:47:48.490
<v Translator>This is what the US wants; this is what Israel wants-- a regime which appears to be democratic to the people, but actually it is this defense national council which will be doing all the work. This council, the National Defense Council, will be the one ruling the country.
<v Translator>这就是美国想要的；这就是以色列想要的——一个看起来对人民民主的政权，但实际上是这个国防国家委员会在做所有工作。这个委员会，国防国家委员会，将是统治这个国家的机构。

00:47:48.490 --> 00:47:56.740
<v Nancy Updike>So are you saying you think President Morsi or part of his government or all of his government are puppets of the United States?
<v Nancy Updike>所以你是说你认为总统穆尔西或他政府的一部分或整个政府是美国的傀儡吗？

00:47:56.740 --> 00:47:58.130
<v Mohammed Agiba>[SPEAKING ARABIC].
<v Mohammed Agiba>[讲阿拉伯语]。

00:47:58.130 --> 00:48:21.360
<v Translator>For sure President Morsi wants the interests of Egypt. However, he sees the implementation of this interest, or finding the interest, from a very narrow perspective that the United States has set for him. We do not want him to see that perspective through the United States' perspective.
<v Translator>总统穆尔西肯定想要埃及的利益。然而，他从美国为他设定的一个非常狭隘的视角来看待这种利益的实现，或寻找这种利益。我们不想让他透过美国的视角来看待那个视角。

00:48:21.360 --> 00:48:35.810
<v Translator>We want him to see it through the Egyptian people's vision and through the Egyptian people's perspective. And we say, please, the Egyptian people should be your priority and not the American administration.
<v Translator>我们想要他透过埃及人民的愿景和埃及人民的视角来看待它。我们说，请，埃及人民应该是你的优先事项，而不是美国政府。

00:48:35.810 --> 00:48:49.930
<v Nancy Updike>I asked Mohammed one more question, not about America, but about Egypt and the choices people have faced there since the revolution. I asked him if he'd voted for Morsi. He did, like a lot of people who are now protesting against Morsi.
<v Nancy Updike>我又问了 Mohammed 一个问题，不是关于美国，而是关于埃及以及自革命以来人们面临的选择。我问他是否投票给了穆尔西。他投了，像许多现在抗议穆尔西的人一样。

00:48:49.930 --> 00:49:06.200
<v Nancy Updike>He said he doesn't regret it. The alternative was worse. He says he just hopes Morsi can pull out of this and pull Egypt out too.
<v Nancy Updike>他说他不后悔。替代方案更糟。他说他只是希望穆尔西能从这一切中退出，并把埃及也拉出来。

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:13.350
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike. She's one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>Nancy Updike。她是我们节目的制片人之一。

00:49:13.350 --> 00:49:20.140
<v Ira Glass>OK, let's go back to America. Friday morning, Boston, Logan Airport, 5:40 AM.
<v Ira Glass>好的，让我们回到美国。周五早上，波士顿，洛根机场，凌晨 5:40。

00:49:20.140 --> 00:49:23.380
<v Fred Beaton>Delta. United.
<v Fred Beaton>达美。联合。

00:49:23.380 --> 00:49:38.060
<v Ira Glass>30 times a night, six nights a week, Fred Beaton drives the shuttle bus on the same loop, from the parking garage to the terminals, picking people up, dropping them off, every 20 minutes, same loop. He's been doing this for 16 years, till this morning. He's retiring.
<v Ira Glass>Fred Beaton 每晚 30 次，每周 6 天，都在相同的回路上驾驶穿梭巴士，从停车场到航站楼，接人、送人，每 20 分钟一次，相同的回路。他做了 16 年了，直到今天早上。他要退休了。

00:49:38.060 --> 00:49:45.220
<v Fred Beaton>Hopefully, this is my last trip around the airport.
<v Fred Beaton>希望这是我最后一次绕机场飞行了。

00:49:45.220 --> 00:49:50.060
<v Vinny>Freddie B, you'll never be done here, never.
<v Vinny>Freddie B，你永远不会在这里结束，永远不会。

00:49:50.060 --> 00:49:53.010
<v Ira Glass>That one was his boss, Vinny, over the radio.
<v Ira Glass>那是他的老板 Vinny，在无线电中说的。

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:54.540
<v Fred Beaton>Off to the sunset.
<v Fred Beaton>奔向夕阳。

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:55.760
<v Ira Glass>But everybody got in on it.
<v Ira Glass>但每个人都参与进来了。

00:49:55.760 --> 00:50:02.130
<v Man On Radio>Freddie B's last ride. [EVIL LAUGH].
<v Man On Radio>Freddie B 的最后一次旅程。[邪恶的笑声]。

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:04.780
<v Ryan Murdoch>So do you feel sad at all, nostalgic, maybe?
<v Ryan Murdoch>所以你感到悲伤、怀旧吗，也许？

00:50:04.780 --> 00:50:07.580
<v Fred Beaton>No, I don't feel sad. No.
<v Fred Beaton>不，我不觉得悲伤。不。

00:50:07.580 --> 00:50:10.280
<v Ryan Murdoch>Not even a little bit?
<v Ryan Murdoch>一点也不？

00:50:10.280 --> 00:50:15.200
<v Fred Beaton>I'm glad, happy. It's somebody else's turn.
<v Fred Beaton>我很高兴，开心。轮到别人了。

00:50:15.200 --> 00:50:31.210
<v Ira Glass>His stepson, Ryan Murdoch, is riding with him, making this recording and talking to him. They calculate that he's done this loop 128,000 times in 16 years. And at 6 o'clock, when he gets to the end of his very last ride, the guy who was supposed to show up was late.
<v Ira Glass>他的继子 Ryan Murdoch 和他一起坐车，进行录音并和他谈话。他们计算出他在 16 年里完成了 128,000 次这个回路。在 6 点钟，当他完成最后一次行程时，本该出现的人迟到了。

00:50:31.210 --> 00:50:41.150
<v Ira Glass>So he has to do one more. Perfect, right? And then he gets to pull the shuttle bus over one last, last time, and climb out.
<v Ira Glass>所以他不得不又做了一次。完美，对吧？然后他终于可以把穿梭巴士最后停下来，爬出去了。

00:50:41.150 --> 00:50:48.880
<v Fred Beaton>All right. Hey, glory Hallelujah. I'll see you.
<v Fred Beaton>好的。嘿，荣耀哈利路亚。再见。

00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:49.890
<v Vinny>Best of luck, Freddie.
<v Vinny>祝好运，Freddie。

00:50:49.890 --> 00:50:50.240
<v Fred Beaton>Take care.
<v Fred Beaton>保重。

00:50:50.240 --> 00:50:51.750
<v Vinny>Nice working with you, my friend.
<v Vinny>很高兴和你一起工作，我的朋友。

00:50:51.750 --> 00:50:53.480
<v Fred Beaton>Yeah.
<v Fred Beaton>是的。

00:50:53.480 --> 00:50:55.460
<v Ryan Murdoch>All right, we're done.
<v Ryan Murdoch>好了，我们结束了。

00:50:55.460 --> 00:50:56.790
<v Fred Beaton>We're done.
<v Fred Beaton>我们结束了。

00:50:56.790 --> 00:50:58.740
<v Ryan Murdoch>You've got a huge smile on your face.
<v Ryan Murdoch>你脸上挂着巨大的笑容。

00:50:58.740 --> 00:51:06.970
<v Fred Beaton>Good. Getting out of  here, you know?
<v Fred Beaton>好极了。要从这里解脱了，你知道吗？

00:51:06.970 --> 00:51:24.070
<v Ira Glass>In Chicago, Linc Cohen began his retirement this week. He worked at a steel mill for 18 years, and then for 20 years after, for the union AFSCME. Monday was his first day home as a retired person. And his wife, Sandi Wisenberg, had some questions. She recorded them.
<v Ira Glass>在芝加哥，Linc Cohen 本周开始了退休生活。他在钢铁厂工作了 18 年，然后为 AFSCME 工会工作了 20 年。周一是他作为退休人员在家的第一天。他的妻子 Sandi Wisenberg 有几个问题。她录了下来。

00:51:24.070 --> 00:51:28.180
<v Sandi Wisenberg>Are you going to make the bed? You can make the bed now.
<v Sandi Wisenberg>你要铺床吗？你现在可以铺床了。

00:51:28.180 --> 00:51:30.660
<v Linc Cohen>Well, you got up after me today.
<v Linc Cohen>嗯，你今天起得比我晚。

00:51:30.660 --> 00:51:31.730
<v Sandi Wisenberg>But I never make it.
<v Sandi Wisenberg>但我从不铺床。

00:51:31.730 --> 00:51:39.700
<v Linc Cohen>I know. When I get up last-- the day before, I got up after you, and I made it.
<v Linc Cohen>我知道。当我最后起时——前一天，我起得比你晚，我就铺了。

00:51:39.700 --> 00:51:43.650
<v Sandi Wisenberg>Right. But what if I get up last, and then you're still here. You can go up and make it.
<v Sandi Wisenberg>对。但如果我最后才起呢，而你还在那儿。你可以去把它铺了。

00:51:43.650 --> 00:51:46.830
<v Linc Cohen>Yeah, I might. I might not.
<v Linc Cohen>是的，我可能会。也可能不会。

00:51:46.830 --> 00:51:52.960
<v Sandi Wisenberg>Oh, I thought you don't like it when it's not made.
<v Sandi Wisenberg>哦，我以为你不喜欢不铺床。

00:51:52.960 --> 00:52:03.340
<v Linc Cohen>I don't like it when it's not made. But I also don't like cleaning up your messes. I have enough of that to do.
<v Linc Cohen>我不喜欢不铺床。但我也不喜欢清理你的烂摊子。我已经有足够多要做的事了。

00:52:03.340 --> 00:52:06.730
<v Linc Cohen>[CELL PHONE ALARM]
<v Linc Cohen>[手机闹钟]

00:52:06.730 --> 00:52:20.080
<v Ira Glass>In other news with people not getting to work in the morning-- Springfield, Oregon, Monday morning of this week, 7:30, a grad student named Morgan Peach hears the alarm that is supposed to wake him up. Then he shuts it off. This happens again at 7:35--
<v Ira Glass>关于人们早上无法上班的其他新闻——俄勒冈州斯普林菲尔德，本周一早上 7:30，一个叫 Morgan Peach 的研究生听到了本该叫醒他的闹钟。然后他关掉了。这在 7:35 又发生了一次——

00:52:20.080 --> 00:52:21.860
<v Ira Glass>[CELL PHONE ALARM]
<v Ira Glass>[手机闹钟]

00:52:21.860 --> 00:52:22.720
<v Ira Glass>--and then again at 7:40.
<v Ira Glass>——然后是 7:40。

00:52:22.720 --> 00:52:24.030
<v Morgan Peach>[GROANS].
<v Morgan Peach>[呻吟声]。

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:26.760
<v Morgan Peach>[CELL PHONE ALARM]
<v Morgan Peach>[手机闹钟]

00:52:26.760 --> 00:52:28.040
<v Ira Glass>Once more at 7:45.
<v Ira Glass>7:45 又来一次。

00:52:28.040 --> 00:52:31.310
<v Ira Glass>[CELL PHONE ALARM]
<v Ira Glass>[手机闹钟]

00:52:31.310 --> 00:52:38.620
<v Ira Glass>You get the idea. 8:15, 8:25. Finally, 9 o'clock, his girlfriend Angela Evancie, who made this recording, steps in.
<v Ira Glass>你明白意思了。8:15，8:25。最后，9 点，他的女朋友 Angela Evancie，制作了这个录音的人，介入了。

00:52:38.620 --> 00:52:39.970
<v Morgan Peach>It's time?
<v Morgan Peach>时间到了？

00:52:39.970 --> 00:52:41.390
<v Angela Jane Evancie>It's time.
<v Angela Jane Evancie>时间到了。

00:52:41.390 --> 00:52:43.850
<v Morgan Peach>It's time.
<v Morgan Peach>时间到了。

00:52:43.850 --> 00:53:01.970
<v Ira Glass>He doesn't get up. It's finals week. Besides an exam, he has two research papers to turn in and 55 exams and papers to grade. Angela sees him struggle every day with waking up. But she thought, this week, this week, for once, he would do it when the alarm sounds, because he has so much to do.
<v Ira Glass>他没起床。这是期末考试周。除了考试，他还有两篇研究论文要交，还有 55 份考试和论文要评分。Angela 每天都看着他挣扎着起床。但她想，这周，这周，破天荒地，他会在闹钟响起时起床，因为他有太多事要做。

00:53:01.970 --> 00:53:15.750
<v Ira Glass>Tuesday, it goes off first at 7:30, then seven more times-- 7:40, 7:45, 8 o'clock, 8:05, 8:10, 8:15, 8:20. Wednesday, pretty much the same deal. At some point, Angela says to her sleeping boyfriend--
<v Ira Glass>周二，闹钟先在 7:30 响起，然后又响了七次——7:40、7:45、8:00、8:05、8:10、8:15、8:20。周三，情况基本相同。在某个时候，Angela 对着熟睡的男朋友说——

00:53:15.750 --> 00:53:18.500
<v Angela Jane Evancie>Did you set the alarm for 9 o'clock?
<v Angela Jane Evancie>你把闹钟设在 9 点了吗？

00:53:18.500 --> 00:53:22.650
<v Morgan Peach>Yes, I did.
<v Morgan Peach>是的，我设了。

00:53:22.650 --> 00:53:25.940
<v Angela Jane Evancie>Can you afford to sleep in that late?
<v Angela Jane Evancie>你起得起这么晚吗？

00:53:25.940 --> 00:53:31.400
<v Morgan Peach>I certainly can. I'm wealthy in time, not in money.
<v Morgan Peach>我绝对可以。我在时间上很富有，钱上不富有。

00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:40.090
<v Ira Glass>Wednesday night, Morgan tells Angela that the next morning, Thursday, he actually is going to have to get up early. A geography paper is due at noon.
<v Ira Glass>周三晚上，Morgan 告诉 Angela，第二天早上，也就是周四，他确实得早起。地理论文中午截止。

00:53:40.090 --> 00:53:54.640
<v Morgan Peach>Yeah, could you set the alarm for 7:30 tomorrow? I'd like to get up at 7:30 and work on my research papers and finish them.
<v Morgan Peach>是的，明天你能把闹钟设在 7:30 吗？我想在 7:30 起床，完成我的研究论文。

00:53:54.640 --> 00:53:56.440
<v Ira Glass>Hours pass.
<v Ira Glass>时间流逝。

00:53:56.440 --> 00:54:11.460
<v Angela Jane Evancie>And now it's 8:00. The alarm went off, and he reset it for 8:30. I really thought he was going to do it this morning.
<v Angela Jane Evancie>现在是 8:00 了。闹钟响了，他把它重设为 8:30。我真的以为他今天早上会起床的。

00:54:11.460 --> 00:54:14.310
<v Ira Glass>Sometime near 10:00 AM, they finally have this conversation.
<v Ira Glass>大约在上午 10:00，他们终于进行了这次对话。

00:54:14.310 --> 00:54:16.520
<v Angela Jane Evancie>It's time to get up.
<v Angela Jane Evancie>是时候起床了。

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:18.430
<v Morgan Peach>[INAUDIBLE].
<v Morgan Peach>[听不清]。

00:54:18.430 --> 00:54:27.100
<v Ira Glass>And then, still lying in bed, he explains the whole thing perfectly. It would be impossible to say this better.
<v Ira Glass>然后，仍然躺在床上，他完美地解释了整件事。如果说得更好是不可能的。

00:54:27.100 --> 00:54:55.810
<v Morgan Peach>It's almost as if the sleeping is that much sweeter when you have to get up or you think you have to get up, and then you don't.
<v Morgan Peach>这几乎就好像，当你必须起床或者你以为你必须起床，然后你又不用起的时候，睡觉感觉特别甜。

00:54:55.810 --> 00:55:11.280
<v Morgan Peach>[MUSIC - "LOVER TO LOVER" BY FLORENCE AND THE MACHINE]
<v Morgan Peach>[音乐 - FLORENCE AND THE MACHINE 的《LOVER TO LOVER》]

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:30.600
<v Ira Glass>Well, our program was produced this week by Sarah Koenig, our senior producer, Julie Snyder, and me, with Alex Blumberg, Ben Calhoun, Jonathan Menjivar, Lisa Pollak, Brian Reed, Robin Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Production help from Tarek Fouda. Amy Phillips, from Pitchfork Media, was our music consultant, finding brand new songs. Seth Lind is our operations director. Elise Bergerson's our administrative assistant.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，我们的节目本周由我们的资深制片人 Sarah Koenig、Julie Snyder 和我制作，参与者还有 Alex Blumberg、Ben Calhoun、Jonathan Menjivar、Lisa Pollak、Brian Reed、Robin Semien、Alissa Shipp 和 Nancy Updike。制作协助来自 Tarek Fouda。来自 Pitchfork Media 的 Amy Phillips 是我们的音乐顾问，负责寻找全新的歌曲。Seth Lind 是我们的运营总监。Elise Bergerson 是我们的行政助理。

00:55:30.600 --> 00:55:46.140
<v Ira Glass>At our website this week, we have a gallery of photos taken on Instagram by people all over the world in these last seven days of events large and small. Emily Condon on our staff did the photo curating. She did so good. You should look. That's at our website, thisamericanlife.org.
<v Ira Glass>在本周我们的网站上，我们有一个 Instagram 照片库，由世界各地的人们在过去七天里拍摄的大大小小的事件组成。我们员工中的 Emily Condon 负责照片策展。她做得太棒了。你应该看看。那是我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。

00:55:46.140 --> 00:56:18.320
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[鸣谢]

00:56:18.320 --> 00:56:35.190
<v Ira Glass>This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International. And our film Sleepwalk With Me is now available for download at iTunes-- itunes.com/sleepwalkwithme. WBEZ management oversight for our program by our boss, Mr. Torey Malatia, who never stops saying--
<v Ira Glass>《美国生活》由公共广播国际发行。我们的电影《与我梦游》（Sleepwalk With Me）现在可以在 iTunes 上下载——itunes.com/sleepwalkwithme。我们节目的 WBEZ 管理层由我们的老板 Torey Malatia 先生监督，他从不停止说——

00:56:35.190 --> 00:56:41.090
<v Hyder Akbar>F This American Life . What the hell are you going to do with This American Life when you're dead?
<v Hyder Akbar>去他妈的《美国生活》。你死后对《美国生活》能做什么？

00:56:41.090 --> 00:57:23.160
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life .
<v Ira Glass>我是 Ira Glass。下周回来，带来更多《美国生活》的故事。

00:57:23.160 --> 00:57:28.160
<v Announcer>PRI, Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI，公共广播国际。
